[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Retrofit Internals and Dynamic Proxies in Android (Java)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Retrofit is one of the most popular HTTP client libraries for Android and Java. At its core, Retrofit turns your HTTP API into a Java interface. Understanding how it achieves this magic through dynamic proxies is crucial for advanced Android engineering."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Role of java.lang.reflect.Proxy"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you call Retrofit.create(MyApi.class), Retrofit does not provide a physical implementation of your interface. Instead, it creates a dynamic proxy using java.lang.reflect.Proxy. This proxy intercepts every method call made on the interface."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"public interface ApiService {
    @GET("users/{id}")
    Call<User> getUser(@Path("id") String userId);
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you invoke apiService.getUser("123"), the Java reflection mechanism routes this call directly to the InvocationHandler associated with the dynamic proxy."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"How Retrofit Processes Method Annotations"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Method Parsing: Retrofit inspects the annotations on the interface method (like @GET, @POST) and parameter annotations (like @Path, @Query, @Body)."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Caching: To avoid performance penalties, Retrofit caches these parsed results in a Map<Method, ServiceMethod<?>> so reflection overhead only occurs once per method."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Request Factory: A ServiceMethod object creates a RequestBuilder for every invocation, translating Java objects into HTTP request parameters."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Writing a Custom Dynamic Proxy Implementation"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To fully grasp Retrofit internals, let us build a simplified custom proxy handler in Java that mimics how Retrofit intercepts method calls."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"import java.lang.reflect.InvocationHandler;
import java.lang.reflect.Method;
import java.lang.reflect.Proxy;

public class MockRetrofitClient {

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public static <T> T create(Class<T> serviceClass) {
        return (T) Proxy.newProxyInstance(
            serviceClass.getClassLoader(),
            new Class<?>[]{serviceClass},
            new InvocationHandler() {
                @Override
                public Object invoke(Object proxy, Method method, Object[] args) throws Throwable {
                    // Intercept method invocation
                    String methodName = method.getName();
                    System.out.println("Intercepted method: " + methodName);

                    // Normally, Retrofit builds an OkHttp Call object here
                    return null;
                }
            }
        );
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Bridging OkHttp and Retrofit Calls"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once the ServiceMethod builds the OkHttp Request, Retrofit wraps it inside an OkHttpCall object. This implements Retrofit's Call interface. When you execute .enqueue() or .execute(), it delegates the actual network I/O down to OkHttp's dispatcher and connection pool."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Retrofit brilliant design relies on Java Dynamic Proxies to map declarative interfaces to imperative HTTP requests. By caching reflection metadata and leveraging OkHttp for execution, it delivers both high developer productivity and runtime efficiency."}]
